/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/az-900.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("az-900", [["azf1",0,1,"A company moves its line-of-business app onto Azure virtual machines. A month later the guest operating system needs security updates. Who is responsible for installing them?",["Microsoft, as the host provider","The hardware vendor of the host","The company that deployed the VMs","Nobody, because Azure VMs self-patch"],2,"In IaaS the customer manages the guest OS, including patches. Microsoft patches the physical hosts and hypervisor, which is why 'Microsoft' is tempting but wrong for the guest OS.","Describe the shared responsibility model",["Microsoft patches the physical hosts and hypervisor, but in IaaS the guest OS inside the VM belongs to the customer.","The hardware vendor has no role in the guest OS; in the cloud Microsoft handles the physical hardware and the customer handles the VM's OS.",null,"Azure VMs do not patch themselves by default; the customer must apply updates or configure a tool such as Azure Update Manager to do it."],2],["azf2",0,1,"Which responsibility stays with the customer whether they use IaaS, PaaS or SaaS?",["Their data and the accounts they own","Physical security of the datacenter","Patching the host hypervisor","Maintaining the physical network"],0,"In every cloud service type the customer keeps responsibility for information and data, devices, and accounts and identities. The physical datacenter, hosts and network are always Microsoft's job in the cloud.","Describe the shared responsibility model",[null,"Physical datacenter security is always Microsoft's job in IaaS, PaaS and SaaS, so it never stays with the customer.","The host hypervisor is part of the physical layer Microsoft manages in every cloud service type.","The physical network is Microsoft's responsibility in all cloud service types, not the customer's."],1],["azf3",0,1,"A hospital must keep its patient records system on hardware it owns, but wants to run its public website in Azure and connect the two. Which cloud model describes this?",["Private cloud","Public cloud","Multicloud","Hybrid cloud"],3,"Hybrid cloud combines private (on-premises) and public cloud resources that work together. Multicloud means using more than one public cloud provider, which is not the case here.","Define cloud models",["A private cloud alone would keep everything on dedicated infrastructure, but the website runs in public Azure too.","Public cloud alone does not fit, because the records system stays on hardware the hospital owns.","Multicloud means using several public cloud providers; here there is one public cloud plus on-premises hardware.",null],3],["azf4",0,1,"An organization runs servers on-premises, in Azure and in another public cloud. It wants to see and govern all of them from the Azure portal with Azure Policy. What should it use?",["Azure Migrate","Azure Arc","ExpressRoute","Azure Advisor"],1,"Azure Arc projects servers and Kubernetes clusters outside Azure into Azure Resource Manager so they can be governed with Policy, RBAC and tags. Azure Migrate is for moving servers into Azure, not managing them where they are.","Describe hybrid and multicloud",["Azure Migrate discovers, assesses and moves servers into Azure; it does not govern servers that stay on-premises or in another cloud.",null,"ExpressRoute is a private network connection to Azure; it provides connectivity, not management or Azure Policy for external servers.","Azure Advisor gives recommendations for Azure resources; it cannot bring non-Azure servers under Azure Policy."],3],["azf5",0,1,"Which statement describes a private cloud?",["Services shared by many tenants over the internet","Resources used exclusively by one organization","A mix of on-premises and public cloud resources","Services bought from several cloud providers"],1,"A private cloud is used by a single organization, on its own premises or hosted by a third party. Sharing among many tenants describes public cloud, and mixing the two is hybrid.","Define cloud models",["Services shared by many tenants over the internet describe a public cloud, not a private one.",null,"Mixing on-premises and public cloud resources is the definition of a hybrid cloud.","Buying services from several cloud providers describes multicloud."],1],["azf6",0,1,"A startup does not want to buy servers up front. It wants to pay each month only for the compute it used. Which type of spending is this?",["Capital expenditure (CapEx)","Depreciation of owned fixed assets","A one-time hardware purchase","Operational expenditure (OpEx)"],3,"Paying as you go for services is OpEx and can be deducted in the same period. CapEx is buying physical assets up front and depreciating them over time.","Compare CapEx and OpEx",["CapEx is up-front spending on assets such as servers, which is exactly what the startup is avoiding.","Depreciation applies to owned assets bought up front; paying monthly for used compute involves no asset to depreciate.","A one-time hardware purchase is CapEx, the opposite of paying monthly only for what was used.",null],2],["azf7",0,1,"An IT manager buys a new storage array for the on-premises datacenter and will depreciate it over five years. How is this cost classified?",["Capital expenditure","Operational expenditure","Consumption-based spending","A pay-as-you-go charge"],0,"An up-front purchase of a physical asset that is depreciated over its life is CapEx. Consumption-based and pay-as-you-go charges are OpEx, which is what the cloud uses.","Compare CapEx and OpEx",[null,"OpEx is ongoing spending on services as they are consumed; buying and depreciating an owned array is not OpEx.","Consumption-based spending means paying for what you use, such as a cloud bill, not buying a device outright.","Pay-as-you-go charges are usage bills for services; a purchased storage array is a one-off asset purchase."],1],["azf8",0,1,"Which is a benefit of the consumption-based model?",["A fixed monthly price whatever you use","Free resources after the first year","No up-front cost; pay only for what you use","Discounts only when buying hardware"],2,"The consumption-based model has no up-front infrastructure cost and you stop paying when you stop using resources. A fixed price regardless of use is the opposite of consumption-based.","Describe the consumption-based model",["A fixed price regardless of use is the opposite of consumption-based pricing, where the bill follows actual usage.","Azure does not make resources free after the first year; the free account's time-limited offers work the other way round.",null,"Discounts tied to buying hardware describe the on-premises model; consumption pricing needs no hardware purchase."],1],["azf9",0,1,"An online store's traffic triples during a holiday sale. It wants web servers added automatically as load grows and removed when it drops. Which cloud benefit is this?",["Fault tolerance","Predictability","Disaster recovery","Elasticity"],3,"Elasticity is automatically scaling resources out and back in with demand, so you pay only for extra capacity while you need it. Fault tolerance is about surviving failures, not load changes.","Describe scalability and elasticity",["Fault tolerance is about continuing to work when a component fails, not adding capacity for more traffic.","Predictability is about forecasting performance and cost; automatic scaling with demand is elasticity.","Disaster recovery restores service after a major outage; it does not add and remove servers with load.",null],3],["azf10",0,1,"A database VM runs short of memory, so an admin resizes it from 2 vCPUs and 8 GB to 8 vCPUs and 32 GB. What kind of scaling is this?",["Scaling out (horizontal)","Scaling up (vertical)","Scaling in (horizontal)","Load balancing"],1,"Giving an existing resource more CPU or memory is vertical scaling, or scaling up. Scaling out would add more VMs instead of making one bigger.","Describe scalability and elasticity",["Scaling out adds more VMs; here a single VM was made bigger.",null,"Scaling in removes instances; the admin added resources to one VM instead.","Load balancing spreads traffic across several instances; it does not change the size of one VM."],2],["azf11",0,1,"What does high availability mean for a cloud service?",["It grows to meet any demand","Its monthly cost can be forecast","It stays accessible despite disruptions","It follows the company's policies"],2,"High availability keeps a service running and reachable despite failures, usually backed by an SLA. Growing to meet demand is scalability, and forecastable cost is predictability.","Describe high availability",["Growing to meet any demand describes scalability, not high availability.","Forecasting monthly cost is cost predictability, which says nothing about staying up during failures.",null,"Following company policies is governance; a compliant service can still go down."],1],["azf12",0,1,"A team compares two Azure services for a critical app and wants the one with the least allowed downtime per month. Which SLA should it prefer?",["99.99%","99%","99.9%","99.95%"],0,"Each extra nine cuts allowed downtime: 99.99% allows about 4 minutes a month, 99.95% about 22 minutes, 99.9% about 43 minutes and 99% over 7 hours.","Describe high availability",[null,"99% allows over 7 hours of downtime a month, the most of these options.","99.9% allows about 43 minutes of downtime a month, more than 99.99% does.","99.95% allows about 22 minutes of downtime a month, still more than 99.99%."],2],["azf13",0,1,"Finance wants to know, before moving to Azure, roughly what the monthly bill will be and to track it once running. Which cloud benefit addresses this?",["Cost predictability","Performance predictability","Elasticity","Geo-distribution"],0,"Cost predictability means forecasting and tracking cloud spend, using tools such as the Pricing Calculator, TCO Calculator and Cost Management. Performance predictability is about how the app performs, not what it costs.","Describe reliability and predictability",[null,"Performance predictability is about the app having the resources it needs, not about forecasting the bill.","Elasticity is automatic scaling with demand; it can change costs but does not help forecast or track them.","Geo-distribution is about deploying close to users in many regions, not estimating or tracking spending."],2],["azf14",0,1,"In cloud computing, what does reliability refer to?",["Paying only for the resources consumed","Letting users sign in with one identity","Recovering from failures and continuing to work","Grouping resources for billing reports"],2,"Reliability is a system's ability to recover from failures and keep functioning, helped by Azure's global scale and redundancy. Paying for what you use is the consumption model.","Describe reliability and predictability",["Paying only for consumed resources is the consumption-based model, not reliability.","Signing in with one identity is single sign-on, an identity feature unrelated to recovering from failures.",null,"Grouping resources for billing reports is cost management, for example with tags or subscriptions."],1],["azf15",0,1,"A development team deploys a web app to Azure App Service. Who applies patches to the underlying operating system and web server?",["The development team","Microsoft","Split equally by contract","A third-party auditor"],1,"App Service is PaaS, so Microsoft manages the OS, runtime and web server; the team manages its code, configuration and data. Patching the OS yourself would apply to IaaS VMs.","Describe the shared responsibility model",["In PaaS the development team manages its code and configuration, not the underlying OS and web server.",null,"Responsibility is not split equally by contract; the shared responsibility model assigns the OS and runtime in PaaS to Microsoft.","Auditors assess compliance; they do not patch any systems."],2],["azf16",0,1,"A company wants to move 30 existing servers to Azure quickly without changing them, and keep full control of each operating system. Which service type fits?",["Software as a service (SaaS)","Platform as a service (PaaS)","Serverless compute with Azure Functions","Infrastructure as a service (IaaS)"],3,"Lift-and-shift with full OS control is the classic IaaS use case: Azure VMs. PaaS removes OS control, which the company wants to keep.","Describe IaaS",["SaaS delivers a finished application, so the company could not bring its own servers or control their OS.","PaaS manages the OS for you, which removes the full OS control the company wants to keep.","Azure Functions runs event-driven code without servers, so it cannot host existing servers unchanged.",null],3],["azf17",0,1,"Developers want to deploy their web API without managing servers, operating systems or runtime updates, but still control their own code. Which service type fits best?",["Infrastructure as a service (IaaS)","Software as a service (SaaS)","Platform as a service (PaaS)","An on-premises cluster"],2,"PaaS gives a managed platform where you bring your code and the provider runs the OS and runtime. SaaS would give a finished app with no code of their own.","Describe PaaS",["IaaS would require the developers to manage the VMs' operating systems and runtime updates, which they want to avoid.","SaaS gives a finished application, so the developers could not deploy their own API code.",null,"An on-premises cluster means buying and managing servers and updates yourself, the opposite of what they want."],2],["azf18",0,1,"A small business wants email, calendars and office apps with no servers or application to maintain. Which option is an example of the right service type?",["Microsoft 365 (SaaS)","Azure Virtual Machines (IaaS)","Azure App Service (PaaS)","Azure Functions (serverless)"],0,"SaaS delivers a complete application managed by the provider; the business only manages its users and data. App Service would still require the business to build and deploy an app.","Describe SaaS",[null,"Azure VMs are IaaS, so the business would have to install and maintain servers and software.","App Service is PaaS for hosting apps you build; the business would still need to develop and run an email application.","Azure Functions runs custom code on events; it does not provide ready-made email, calendars or office apps."],1],["azf19",0,1,"A function resizes images whenever a file is uploaded and sits idle the rest of the time. What is a key benefit of running it serverless?",["Reserving a fixed VM size","Managing the OS and its patches","A flat monthly fee per server","Billing only while the code runs"],3,"Serverless compute such as Azure Functions on the Consumption plan bills for executions and run time, and scales automatically. Reserving VM sizes and patching the OS are IaaS traits.","Describe serverless",["Reserving a fixed VM size is an IaaS cost option and means paying for capacity even while the code is idle.","Managing the OS and patches is an IaaS responsibility that serverless removes, not a benefit of it.","A flat monthly fee per server is how you pay for always-on capacity, not for code that runs only on uploads.",null],3],["azf20",0,1,"A small company has no staff to guard server rooms, control badge access or replace failed disks. Which cloud benefit helps most?",["The company still guards its own racks","Microsoft secures the physical datacenters","Physical security is no longer needed","Customers visit datacenters to audit racks"],1,"In every cloud service type the provider handles physical security of datacenters, hosts and network. Physical security is still needed; it just becomes Microsoft's responsibility.","Describe security benefits of the cloud",["Guarding its own racks is what the company cannot do; in the cloud there are no customer racks to guard.",null,"Physical security is still essential; it simply becomes Microsoft's responsibility in the cloud.","Customers generally cannot walk into Azure datacenters to audit racks; they rely on Microsoft's independent audit reports."],2],["azf21",0,1,"An organization uses Azure for its web apps and another public cloud provider for its analytics, at the same time. What is this called?",["Hybrid cloud","Multicloud","Private cloud","Community hosting"],1,"Using services from more than one public cloud provider is multicloud. Hybrid cloud specifically combines private or on-premises infrastructure with a public cloud.","Define cloud models",["Hybrid cloud combines private or on-premises infrastructure with public cloud; here both environments are public clouds.",null,"A private cloud is dedicated to one organization, not a mix of two public providers.","Community hosting is not a standard cloud model and does not describe using two public providers at once."],1],["azf22",0,1,"A tester can create a full test environment in the portal in minutes, without raising a hardware purchase request, and delete it the same afternoon. Which cloud characteristic does this show?",["Capital expenditure planning","Vendor lock-in","Physical isolation of hardware","On-demand self-service"],3,"Cloud resources can be provisioned by the user on demand and released just as quickly. Capital expenditure is the purchase model this avoids.","Describe cloud computing",["Capital expenditure planning involves purchase requests and hardware budgets, which the tester skipped entirely.","Vendor lock-in is the difficulty of moving between providers, not the ability to create resources quickly.","Physical isolation of hardware relates to private or dedicated hosting, not to creating and deleting resources in minutes.",null],3],["azf23",0,1,"A company uses a SaaS customer relationship app. Which of these is still the company's responsibility?",["The data and user accounts in the app","Patching the application code","The datacenter network","The server operating system"],0,"Even in SaaS the customer owns its information, devices and accounts and identities. The provider patches the app, OS and infrastructure.","Describe the shared responsibility model",[null,"In SaaS the provider patches the application code, not the customer.","The datacenter network is always Microsoft's or the SaaS provider's responsibility in the cloud.","The server OS in SaaS is managed entirely by the provider."],2],["azf24",0,1,"A company wants rules that block noncompliant deployments and report on existing resources that break its standards. Which cloud benefit is this?",["Elasticity","Consumption-based pricing","Governance","Geo-distribution"],2,"Governance in the cloud means defining and enforcing standards, for example with Azure Policy and templates, and auditing compliance. Elasticity is about scaling with demand.","Describe governance benefits of the cloud",["Elasticity is automatically scaling resources with demand, not enforcing standards.","Consumption-based pricing is how you pay for resources; it does not block noncompliant deployments.",null,"Geo-distribution means deploying close to users in many regions, not enforcing or reporting on standards."],3],["azf25",0,2,"A company's app runs on VMs in one Azure region and must keep working if a single datacenter in that region loses power. What should it do?",["Use one larger VM size","Add tags to each VM","Move the disks to Premium SSD","Spread the VMs across availability zones"],3,"Availability zones are physically separate datacenters within a region with independent power, cooling and networking, so a zone failure leaves VMs in other zones running. A larger VM is still a single point of failure.","Describe availability zones",["A larger VM is still one VM in one datacenter, so a power loss there still takes it down.","Tags are metadata for organizing and reporting; they add no protection against a datacenter failure.","Premium SSD disks improve disk performance but still sit in the same datacenter as the VM.",null],3],["azf26",0,2,"What is a benefit of Azure region pairs?",["Both regions share one physical datacenter","Planned updates roll out to one region of a pair at a time","Every service copies all data between them","Traffic between them is never charged"],1,"Microsoft sequences platform updates across a pair and prioritizes recovering one region of each pair during a broad outage. Not every service replicates to the pair automatically; only some, such as geo-redundant storage, do.","Describe regions and region pairs",["Paired regions are in separate locations, often hundreds of miles apart; they do not share a datacenter.",null,"Only some services, such as geo-redundant storage, replicate to the pair; most do not copy data automatically.","Data transfer between regions, including paired ones, is generally billed."],2],["azf27",0,2,"A US federal agency needs Azure services in a physically and logically isolated instance that is operated by screened US personnel. What should it use?",["A region pair in the US","An availability zone","Azure Government","A management group"],2,"Azure Government is a sovereign region for US government agencies and partners, isolated from global Azure. Region pairs and availability zones are part of the normal public cloud.","Describe sovereign regions",["A US region pair is part of public global Azure, not an isolated government instance.","An availability zone is a group of datacenters within a normal region; it gives no sovereign isolation or screened staff.",null,"A management group organizes subscriptions for governance; it does not change where or by whom the cloud is operated."],2],["azf28",0,2,"A project uses a web app, a database and a storage account that are created and deleted together. How should they be organized?",["Put them in one resource group","Put each in its own subscription","Apply a CanNotDelete lock","Create a management group for them"],0,"A resource group holds resources that share a lifecycle, so they can be managed, secured and deleted together. A lock would prevent the deletion the team wants.","Describe resource groups",[null,"Separate subscriptions add billing and management overhead and make it harder to manage the three resources as one unit.","A CanNotDelete lock prevents deletion, but the team wants to delete the resources together.","Management groups contain subscriptions, not individual resources, so they cannot group these three items."],1],["azf29",0,2,"A company needs separate invoices and spending limits for its Development and Production environments. What is the most direct way to separate billing?",["Use separate subscriptions","Use separate resource groups","Use separate availability zones","Use separate virtual networks"],0,"Subscriptions are a billing boundary, so each environment gets its own invoice and cost reporting. Resource groups can be tracked with tags but share one subscription bill.","Describe subscriptions",[null,"Resource groups in one subscription share one invoice; they can be reported on but are not separate billing boundaries.","Availability zones are physical locations for resilience; they have nothing to do with invoices.","Virtual networks are network boundaries, not billing boundaries."],2],["azf30",0,2,"A company has 25 subscriptions and wants the same Azure Policy and role assignments applied to all of them at once. What should it use?",["A resource group","A resource lock","A management group","An availability set"],2,"Management groups sit above subscriptions, and policies and RBAC assigned there are inherited by every subscription below. Resource groups sit below subscriptions and cannot contain them.","Describe management groups",["Resource groups sit inside subscriptions and cannot contain them, so they cannot cover 25 subscriptions.","A resource lock prevents deletion or changes; it cannot apply policies or role assignments.",null,"An availability set spreads VMs across fault and update domains; it is unrelated to governance."],3],["azf31",0,2,"Which list shows the Azure hierarchy from the top down?",["Subscriptions, management groups, resources, resource groups","Management groups, subscriptions, resource groups, resources","Resource groups, subscriptions, management groups, resources","Resources, resource groups, management groups, subscriptions"],1,"Management groups contain subscriptions, subscriptions contain resource groups, and resource groups contain resources. Settings applied higher up are inherited by lower levels.","Describe the Azure hierarchy",["Management groups sit above subscriptions, and resource groups sit above resources, so this order is wrong on both counts.",null,"Resource groups are near the bottom, inside subscriptions, not at the top of the hierarchy.","This starts with resources, which are the bottom level, and even read bottom-up it wrongly puts management groups below subscriptions."],1],["azf32",0,2,"A company must run a legacy application that needs a specific Windows Server version and a custom driver installed. Which compute option fits?",["Azure Functions","Azure App Service","Azure Container Instances","Azure Virtual Machines"],3,"VMs give full control of the operating system, so custom drivers and software can be installed. Functions and App Service are PaaS and do not allow OS-level changes.","Describe virtual machines",["Azure Functions runs short event-driven code on a managed platform, with no way to install custom drivers.","App Service is PaaS; you cannot pick an arbitrary Windows Server version or install OS-level drivers.","Container Instances runs container images that share a host kernel; installing a custom kernel-level driver is not possible.",null],2],["azf33",0,2,"A web tier needs many identical VMs that are added automatically when CPU is high and removed when it is low. What should be used?",["Availability sets across fault domains","Azure Virtual Desktop","Virtual Machine Scale Sets","Resource locks"],2,"Scale sets manage a group of identical, load-balanced VMs and can autoscale on metrics. Availability sets spread VMs across fault and update domains but do not add or remove VMs.","Describe virtual machine scale sets",["Availability sets spread VMs across fault and update domains for resilience but do not add or remove VMs automatically.","Azure Virtual Desktop delivers desktops to users; it is not for autoscaling a web tier.",null,"Resource locks prevent deletion or changes; they do not create or remove VMs."],2],["azf34",0,2,"Staff need a Windows desktop with company apps that they can reach from home laptops, tablets or a browser, while data stays in Azure. Which service fits?",["Azure Virtual Desktop","Azure Container Apps","Azure Bastion with native client","Azure App Service"],0,"Azure Virtual Desktop delivers desktops and apps from Azure to almost any device. Bastion gives admins browser-based RDP or SSH to VMs, not desktops for end users.","Describe Azure Virtual Desktop",[null,"Container Apps hosts containerized microservices, not interactive Windows desktops for staff.","Azure Bastion gives administrators secure RDP or SSH to VMs, not a managed desktop service for end users.","App Service hosts web applications and APIs, not full Windows desktops with company apps."],2],["azf35",0,2,"A developer wants to run one container image for a short batch job without setting up VMs or an orchestrator, and pay only while it runs. What should they use?",["Azure Kubernetes Service","Virtual Machine Scale Sets","Azure Virtual Desktop","Azure Container Instances"],3,"Container Instances is the fastest, simplest way to run a container in Azure with per-second billing and no servers to manage. AKS is for orchestrating many containers and adds cluster management.","Describe containers",["AKS is an orchestrator for running many containers on a cluster, which is more setup than one short job needs.","Scale sets manage groups of VMs, which the developer specifically wants to avoid.","Azure Virtual Desktop delivers desktops to users and does not run container batch jobs.",null],2],["azf36",0,2,"A company runs dozens of microservices in containers and needs full Kubernetes features such as custom controllers and node pools. Which service fits?",["Azure Container Instances","Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS)","Azure Functions","Azure Batch with low-priority VMs"],1,"AKS is managed Kubernetes for orchestrating many containers at scale with full Kubernetes API access. Container Instances runs individual container groups with no orchestration.","Describe containers",["Container Instances runs individual container groups without orchestration, custom controllers or node pools.",null,"Azure Functions runs event-driven code, not Kubernetes workloads with custom controllers.","Azure Batch schedules large parallel compute jobs; it does not provide the Kubernetes API or controllers."],2],["azf37",0,2,"A team needs code that runs each time a message arrives in a queue, with no servers to manage and automatic scaling. Which service fits best?",["Azure Virtual Machines","Azure Functions","Azure Virtual Desktop","Azure Container Registry"],1,"Azure Functions is event-driven serverless compute: triggers such as queue messages start the code, and it scales automatically. VMs would need to run and be paid for all the time.","Describe Azure Functions",["VMs would run, and be billed, all the time and require server management, which the team wants to avoid.",null,"Azure Virtual Desktop provides user desktops and does not run code in response to queue messages.","Container Registry stores container images; it does not run code when messages arrive."],2],["azf38",0,2,"A company wants to host a .NET web application with built-in autoscale, deployment slots and managed patching, without containers. Which service fits?",["Azure Kubernetes Service","Azure Container Instances","Azure Virtual Machines","Azure App Service"],3,"App Service is a PaaS for web apps and APIs in languages such as .NET, Java, Node.js and Python, with the platform managing the OS. AKS would require building containers and managing a cluster.","Describe application hosting options",["AKS would require packaging the app in containers and managing a cluster, which the company wants to avoid.","Container Instances runs containers, which the company explicitly does not want, and lacks deployment slots.","VMs would leave OS patching and scaling setup to the company rather than providing managed patching.",null],2],["azf39",0,2,"VMs in two Azure virtual networks must talk to each other privately over the Microsoft backbone without gateways. What should be configured?",["Virtual network peering","A site-to-site VPN","An ExpressRoute circuit","A public IP on each VM"],0,"Peering connects VNets so traffic stays on Microsoft's backbone network using private IPs. A site-to-site VPN is for linking an on-premises network, not two VNets in Azure.","Describe virtual networks",[null,"A site-to-site VPN links an on-premises network to Azure through gateways, which the requirement rules out.","ExpressRoute is a private connection from on-premises to Azure through a provider, not a way to link two VNets without gateways.","Public IPs would send traffic over public addresses instead of privately over the backbone."],2],["azf40",0,2,"A branch office needs an encrypted connection from its on-premises firewall to an Azure virtual network over the public internet. What should be deployed?",["An ExpressRoute circuit","Virtual network peering","A site-to-site VPN gateway","A point-to-site VPN client"],2,"A VPN gateway with a site-to-site connection creates an IPsec tunnel between an on-premises VPN device and Azure over the internet. ExpressRoute is a private connection that does not use the public internet.","Describe VPN Gateway",["ExpressRoute is a private circuit through a connectivity provider and does not travel over the public internet.","VNet peering connects Azure virtual networks to each other, not an on-premises office to Azure.",null,"Point-to-site VPN connects individual devices, not an entire office network through its firewall."],2],["azf41",0,2,"A bank needs a private, dedicated connection from its datacenter to Azure that does not travel over the public internet and offers predictable latency. What should it use?",["Point-to-site VPN","Virtual network peering","Azure DNS private zones","ExpressRoute"],3,"ExpressRoute provides private connectivity through a connectivity provider, bypassing the public internet. VPN connections are encrypted but still use the internet.","Describe ExpressRoute",["Point-to-site VPN connects single devices over the public internet, which the bank must avoid.","VNet peering connects Azure VNets together; it does not connect an on-premises datacenter.","Azure DNS private zones provide name resolution inside VNets and create no network connection.",null],3],["azf42",0,2,"A company wants to host the DNS records for its domain contoso.com in Azure, managed with the same credentials and billing as its other resources. Which service fits?",["Azure Front Door","Azure DNS","Azure Firewall","Microsoft Entra ID"],1,"Azure DNS hosts DNS zones on Azure's global name servers so records can be managed with Azure tools and RBAC. It does not sell domain names; you delegate a domain you already own.","Describe Azure DNS",["Azure Front Door is a global entry point for web traffic with load balancing and caching, not a DNS hosting service.",null,"Azure Firewall filters network traffic; it does not host a domain's DNS records.","Microsoft Entra ID is an identity service; verifying a domain there does not host its DNS zone."],2],["azf43",0,2,"An app in a virtual network must reach a storage account over a private IP address, with no traffic using the storage account's public endpoint. What should be created?",["A public IP address","A point-to-site VPN","A private endpoint","A resource lock"],2,"A private endpoint gives a PaaS resource a private IP in your VNet so traffic stays on the Microsoft network. Public IPs and the public endpoint are what the requirement avoids.","Describe public and private endpoints",["A public IP address would expose the connection on a public endpoint, the opposite of the requirement.","Point-to-site VPN connects individual client devices into a VNet; it does not give the storage account a private IP.",null,"A resource lock protects against deletion or changes and has no effect on network paths."],3],["azf44",0,2,"A company is moving a file server to Azure and wants users to keep mapping a network drive over SMB. Which storage service fits?",["Azure Files","Azure Blob Storage","Azure Queue Storage","Azure Disk Storage"],0,"Azure Files offers fully managed file shares reached with SMB or NFS that can be mapped as drives. Blob Storage is object storage without SMB access.","Describe Azure Storage services",[null,"Blob Storage is object storage accessed over HTTP(S) APIs and cannot be mapped as an SMB network drive.","Queue Storage holds messages between application components, not files for users.","Azure Disks are block volumes attached to VMs, not shares users can map directly."],2],["azf45",0,2,"Scanned contracts must be kept for 7 years, are almost never read, and the company can wait several hours to retrieve one. Which access tier costs least to store them?",["Archive","Hot","Cool","Cold"],0,"Archive has the lowest storage cost but data is offline and must be rehydrated, which can take hours. Cool and Cold keep data online and cost more to store.","Describe storage access tiers",[null,"Hot has the highest storage cost and is meant for frequently accessed data, the opposite of this case.","Cool is online and costs more to store than Archive; paying for instant access is unnecessary here.","Cold is still an online tier with higher storage cost than Archive, and the company can wait hours."],2],["azf46",0,2,"A storage account must let an app read data from a secondary region at any time, even when the primary region is healthy. Which redundancy option fits?",["LRS","ZRS","RA-GRS","GRS"],2,"Read-access geo-redundant storage replicates to the paired region and allows reads from the secondary endpoint at any time. Plain GRS replicates too but the secondary is only readable after a failover.","Describe storage redundancy",["LRS keeps all copies in one datacenter in the primary region, with no secondary region to read from.","ZRS spreads copies across zones in the primary region only, with no secondary region.",null,"GRS replicates to a secondary region, but that copy is readable only after a failover, not at any time."],3],["azf47",0,2,"Data must survive the loss of one datacenter in a region but, for residency rules, must never be copied to another region. Which redundancy option fits?",["Locally redundant storage (LRS)","Zone-redundant storage (ZRS)","Geo-redundant storage (GRS)","Geo-zone-redundant storage (GZRS)"],1,"ZRS keeps three copies across availability zones in the same region. LRS keeps all copies in one datacenter, and GRS or GZRS copy data to a second region.","Describe storage redundancy",["LRS keeps all three copies in a single datacenter, so the loss of that datacenter could make the data unavailable.",null,"GRS copies data to a second region, which breaks the residency rule.","GZRS also replicates to a secondary region, which the residency rule forbids."],3],["azf48",0,2,"A company must move 80 TB of archives to Azure, but its internet link would take months to upload them. What should it use?",["AzCopy over the internet","Azure File Sync","Azure Storage Explorer","Azure Data Box"],3,"Data Box is a physical device Microsoft ships to you; you load the data and ship it back for upload into Azure. AzCopy and Storage Explorer still depend on the slow link.","Describe data migration options",["AzCopy still uploads over the same slow internet link, so it would take months.","Azure File Sync continuously syncs file servers with Azure Files over the network; it does not solve a bulk transfer over a slow link.","Storage Explorer is a graphical tool that uses the network to upload, so it faces the same slow link.",null],2],["azf49",0,2,"Before moving 200 on-premises servers to Azure, a company wants to discover them, assess readiness and size, and then migrate them from one hub. What should it use?",["Azure Arc","Azure Data Box","Azure Migrate","Azure Advisor"],2,"Azure Migrate provides discovery, assessment and migration tools in one place. Azure Arc manages servers where they already are rather than moving them.","Describe Azure Migrate",["Azure Arc manages servers where they already run; it does not assess or migrate them into Azure.","Data Box moves large amounts of data offline; it does not discover or assess servers.",null,"Azure Advisor makes recommendations for resources already in Azure, not assessments of on-premises servers."],2],["azf50",0,2,"Branch offices want to keep a local Windows file server for fast access while the full set of files is stored centrally in an Azure file share. What should be used?",["Azure File Sync","Azure Data Box","Azure Migrate","Azure Blob tiering"],0,"Azure File Sync caches an Azure file share on Windows Servers, keeping frequently used files local and tiering the rest to the cloud. Data Box is a one-time offline transfer device.","Describe file movement options",[null,"Data Box is a one-time offline transfer device, not an ongoing sync with local caching.","Azure Migrate moves servers into Azure; it does not keep a local file server synchronized with a file share.","Blob access tiers move blobs between Hot, Cool, Cold and Archive; they do not cache files on a Windows file server."],2],["azf51",0,2,"Employees sign in once in the morning and can then open Microsoft 365, the HR portal and other connected apps without signing in again. Which feature provides this?",["Multifactor authentication","Conditional Access","Role-based access control","Single sign-on (SSO)"],3,"SSO lets one identity and one sign-in give access to many applications. MFA adds verification factors at sign-in but does not remove the need to sign in to each app.","Describe authentication methods",["MFA adds a second verification step at sign-in; it does not remove the need to sign in to each app.","Conditional Access decides whether to allow or add requirements to a sign-in; it does not provide one sign-in for many apps.","RBAC controls what users may do on Azure resources, not how many times they sign in.",null],2],["azf52",0,2,"After entering a password, users must also approve a notification in the Microsoft Authenticator app before they can sign in. What is this?",["Single sign-on","Multifactor authentication","Passwordless sign-in with FIDO2 keys","Azure RBAC"],1,"Requiring a second factor, such as something you have (a phone app), in addition to something you know is MFA. Passwordless sign-in removes the password altogether, which does not happen here.","Describe authentication methods",["Single sign-on reduces how often users sign in; it does not add a second verification factor.",null,"Passwordless removes the password entirely, but here users still enter a password first.","Azure RBAC authorizes actions on resources after sign-in; it does not add verification to sign-in."],1],["azf53",0,2,"A company wants to require MFA only when users sign in from outside the corporate network or from an unmanaged device. What should it configure?",["Azure Policy location rules","Conditional Access policies","Resource locks","Azure DNS private zones"],1,"Conditional Access evaluates signals such as location, device and risk to decide whether to allow, block or require MFA. Azure Policy governs resource settings, not user sign-in.","Describe Conditional Access",["Azure Policy controls resource configurations such as allowed regions; it does not evaluate user sign-ins.",null,"Resource locks protect resources from deletion or change and have nothing to do with sign-in requirements.","Azure DNS private zones resolve names inside VNets and cannot require MFA."],3],["azf54",0,2,"Consultants from a partner company need to access an internal app using their own company credentials instead of new accounts. What should be used?",["Microsoft Entra Domain Services","Microsoft Entra Connect Sync","A shared admin account","B2B collaboration in Entra External ID"],3,"B2B collaboration invites external users as guests who sign in with their own organization's identity. Domain Services provides managed domain features such as LDAP and Kerberos, not partner access.","Describe external identities",["Entra Domain Services provides a managed domain with LDAP and Kerberos, not access for partner users with their own credentials.","Entra Connect Sync copies on-premises AD identities to Entra ID; it does not bring in another company's users.","A shared admin account breaks accountability and least privilege and still is not the consultants' own credentials.",null],3],["azf55",0,2,"A legacy app moved to Azure VMs needs domain join, LDAP and Kerberos authentication, but the company does not want to deploy or patch domain controllers. What should it use?",["Microsoft Entra Domain Services","Microsoft Entra B2B collaboration","Azure DNS private zones","Conditional Access policies"],0,"Entra Domain Services provides managed domain services such as domain join, Group Policy, LDAP and Kerberos without running your own domain controllers. B2B is for inviting external users.","Describe Microsoft Entra Domain Services",[null,"B2B collaboration invites external guest users; it does not provide domain join, LDAP or Kerberos.","Azure DNS private zones provide name resolution only, not domain services or authentication.","Conditional Access controls sign-ins to Entra ID apps; it does not offer LDAP, Kerberos or domain join."],3],["azf56",0,2,"Help desk staff must be able to restart VMs in one resource group but not delete them or change other resources. What is the right approach?",["Apply a ReadOnly lock to the group","Create an Azure Policy initiative","Assign a suitable RBAC role at that scope","Add a tag to each VM"],2,"Azure RBAC grants specific actions to users or groups at a scope, which is how least privilege is applied. A ReadOnly lock would block restarts too and would not grant any access.","Describe Azure RBAC",["A ReadOnly lock would block restarts and grant no permissions, so the help desk still could not act.","A policy initiative controls which resource configurations are allowed, not which actions users may perform.",null,"Tags are metadata and grant no permissions to anyone."],3],["azf57",0,2,"Which statement reflects the Zero Trust model?",["Trust devices inside the network","Grant broad access to cut tickets","Rely on the perimeter firewall","Verify explicitly and assume breach"],3,"Zero Trust's principles are verify explicitly, use least-privilege access and assume breach. Trusting anything inside the corporate network is the older perimeter model Zero Trust replaces.","Describe Zero Trust",["Trusting devices because they are inside the network is the old perimeter model that Zero Trust rejects.","Broad access contradicts Zero Trust's least-privilege principle, whatever it saves in tickets.","Relying on a perimeter firewall assumes the inside is safe, which Zero Trust's 'assume breach' principle rejects.",null],1],["azf58",0,2,"A security team wants a secure score, hardening recommendations and threat protection for resources in Azure, on-premises and in other clouds. Which service fits?",["Microsoft Purview","Microsoft Defender for Cloud","Azure Service Health","Microsoft Cost Management reports"],1,"Defender for Cloud assesses security posture across Azure, hybrid and multicloud resources and adds workload threat protection. Purview governs data, not the security posture of resources.","Describe Microsoft Defender for Cloud",["Purview governs and classifies data; it does not provide a secure score or threat protection for resources.",null,"Service Health reports Azure platform incidents and maintenance, not security posture.","Cost Management reports on spending and has no security recommendations or threat protection."],2],["azf59",0,2,"A company protects data with physical security, identity controls, network segmentation, host hardening and encryption, so an attacker who gets past one control meets the next. What approach is this?",["Single sign-on","Shared responsibility","Defense in depth","Elastic scaling"],2,"Defense in depth uses layers of security so that no single failed control exposes the data. Shared responsibility describes who manages what, not layered controls.","Describe defense in depth",["Single sign-on is one identity convenience feature, not a strategy of layered controls.","Shared responsibility describes who manages which layer, provider or customer, not stacking controls against attackers.",null,"Elastic scaling adds and removes resources with demand and has nothing to do with layered security."],2],["azf60",0,3,"A team prices the same VM size in two regions and gets different hourly rates. What explains this?",["Prices can vary by region","Prices vary by resource group name","The number of tags changes the rate","One region is always free for a year"],0,"Azure prices for the same service can differ between regions because of local costs and taxes. Resource group names and tags do not affect price.","Describe factors that affect costs",[null,"A resource group's name is just a label and has no effect on pricing.","Tags are metadata for organizing and reporting; they do not change a resource's rate.","No Azure region is always free for a year; free offers are account-level, not tied to one region."],1],["azf61",0,3,"Which kind of network traffic is generally not charged in Azure?",["Data transferred into Azure","Data sent out to the internet","Data sent between two regions","Data sent to another cloud"],0,"Inbound data transfer (ingress) into Azure datacenters is generally free. Outbound data (egress) to the internet or another region is billed, usually after a small free amount.","Describe factors that affect costs",[null,"Outbound data to the internet (egress) is billed, usually after a small free amount.","Data transfer between Azure regions is billed.","Sending data to another cloud is outbound transfer from Azure, which is billed."],1],["azf62",0,3,"A database VM will run 24 hours a day for the next three years. How can the company lower its compute cost the most without changing the VM?",["Move it to a new resource group","Add a CanNotDelete lock","Buy a 3-year reserved instance","Switch it to a Spot VM"],2,"Reservations give a large discount for committing to 1 or 3 years of steady usage. Spot VMs are cheap but can be evicted at any time, which does not suit an always-on database.","Describe factors that affect costs",["Moving a VM to another resource group changes its organization, not its price.","A CanNotDelete lock protects the VM from deletion but has no effect on cost.",null,"Spot VMs can be evicted whenever Azure needs capacity, which is unsuitable for an always-on database."],3],["azf63",0,3,"A company already owns Windows Server licenses with active Software Assurance. How can it use them to reduce the cost of Azure VMs?",["The TCO Calculator","Azure Hybrid Benefit","A free trial extension","Azure Reservations only"],1,"Azure Hybrid Benefit lets you apply eligible existing Windows Server and SQL Server licenses to Azure, so you pay only the base compute rate. The TCO Calculator estimates savings but does not apply any discount.","Describe factors that affect costs",["The TCO Calculator estimates savings from migrating; it does not apply any license discount.",null,"There is no free trial extension that lets you apply owned licenses to reduce VM costs.","Reservations discount compute for a time commitment but do not use the licenses the company already owns."],2],["azf64",0,3,"Before deploying anything, a project lead wants a monthly estimate for 4 VMs, a SQL database and 1 TB of storage in Azure. Which tool should they use?",["The TCO Calculator","Cost analysis in Cost Management","Azure Advisor","The Pricing Calculator"],3,"The Pricing Calculator estimates the cost of Azure services you plan to use. The TCO Calculator compares on-premises costs with Azure, and Cost analysis shows spend that has already happened.","Describe the Pricing Calculator",["The TCO Calculator compares on-premises costs with Azure over years; it is not for pricing a planned set of Azure services.","Cost analysis shows spending that has already happened, so it cannot estimate resources not yet deployed.","Azure Advisor recommends improvements for existing resources; it does not produce estimates for new ones.",null],2],["azf65",0,3,"An executive asks how much the company would save over five years by moving its datacenter's servers, including power, cooling and IT labor, to Azure. Which tool answers this?",["The Pricing Calculator","Azure Service Health","The TCO Calculator","Azure Monitor"],2,"The Total Cost of Ownership Calculator compares the full cost of running workloads on-premises with running them in Azure over time. The Pricing Calculator only prices Azure services.","Describe the TCO Calculator",["The Pricing Calculator prices Azure services only and does not include on-premises costs like power, cooling and labor.","Service Health reports Azure incidents and maintenance, not cost comparisons.",null,"Azure Monitor collects metrics and logs; it does not model migration savings."],3],["azf66",0,3,"A team wants an email when its subscription reaches 80% of a $500 monthly spending limit. What should it configure?",["A budget in Cost Management","An Azure Policy assignment","A resource lock","An Azure Service Health alert rule"],0,"Budgets in Microsoft Cost Management send alerts when actual or forecast spend reaches thresholds you set. Budgets alert but do not stop resources, and Service Health alerts are about platform incidents.","Describe Cost Management",[null,"Azure Policy governs resource configurations; it does not track spending or send budget alerts.","A resource lock prevents deletion or changes and knows nothing about spending.","Service Health alerts notify about Azure incidents and maintenance, not about your spending."],2],["azf67",0,3,"Finance wants to split one subscription's bill by department. Resources for several departments share resource groups. What should be applied to the resources?",["Resource locks","Policy exemptions","Separate availability zones per team","Tags such as CostCenter"],3,"Tags are name-value pairs that appear in cost reports, so costs can be grouped and filtered by department. Locks and zones have nothing to do with cost reporting.","Describe the purpose of tags",["Resource locks prevent deletion or changes; they do not label costs by department.","Policy exemptions exclude resources from a policy assignment; they do not appear as a cost grouping.","Availability zones are physical locations for resilience and would not split a bill by department.",null],3],["azf68",0,3,"An admin adds the tag Environment=Prod to a resource group. What happens to the resources already inside it?",["They all get the tag immediately","They do not receive the tag automatically","They get it at the next billing cycle","They lose any tags they already had"],1,"Tags are not inherited from resource groups or subscriptions. To copy a group's tag to its resources automatically, use Azure Policy, such as the built-in 'Inherit a tag from the resource group' definition.","Describe the purpose of tags",["Tags on a resource group are not inherited automatically by the resources inside it.",null,"Billing cycles do not copy tags; inheritance would need Azure Policy or a Cost Management setting.","Adding a tag to the group does not touch the resources' existing tags."],2],["azf69",0,3,"A company must discover and classify sensitive data across on-premises SQL servers, Azure storage and SaaS apps, and see where it flows. Which service fits?",["Microsoft Defender for Cloud","Microsoft Purview","Azure Service Health","Azure Policy"],1,"Microsoft Purview provides data governance, risk and compliance: mapping, classifying and tracking data across the estate. Defender for Cloud focuses on the security posture of resources, not data cataloging.","Describe Microsoft Purview",["Defender for Cloud assesses and protects the security posture of resources; it does not catalog or classify data across the estate.",null,"Service Health reports Azure platform incidents and has nothing to do with data classification.","Azure Policy enforces resource configuration rules; it does not discover sensitive data or map its flow."],2],["azf70",0,3,"Company rules say resources may only be deployed to two approved regions, even by subscription owners. What should be used?",["Azure RBAC","Resource tags","Azure Advisor","Azure Policy"],3,"Azure Policy can deny resources that do not meet rules, such as the built-in Allowed locations definition, regardless of who deploys them. RBAC controls who can act, not where resources may go.","Describe Azure Policy",["RBAC controls who can perform actions; an Owner could still deploy to any region.","Tags are labels and cannot block a deployment to a disallowed region.","Azure Advisor makes recommendations; it cannot prevent deployments.",null],3],["azf71",0,3,"A compliance team wants to group 15 related policy definitions for a security standard and assign them together with one compliance view. What should it create?",["An initiative","A resource group","A management lock","A budget alert"],0,"An initiative (policy set) groups policy definitions so they can be assigned and tracked as one unit. A resource group only organizes resources.","Describe Azure Policy",[null,"A resource group organizes resources; it cannot group policy definitions.","A management lock prevents deletion or changes; it does not bundle policies or provide a compliance view.","A budget alert is about spending, not grouping policy definitions."],2],["azf72",0,3,"A production database must be protected from accidental deletion, but admins still need to change its settings. What should be applied?",["A ReadOnly resource lock","A Reader role assignment","A CanNotDelete resource lock","A policy with the Audit effect"],2,"CanNotDelete blocks deletion while allowing reads and changes. ReadOnly would also block setting changes, and an Audit policy only reports.","Describe resource locks",["A ReadOnly lock would also block changes to the database's settings, which admins still need to make.","A Reader role assignment grants view-only access; it does not protect against other people deleting the database.",null,"An Audit policy only reports noncompliance; it would not stop anyone deleting the database."],3],["azf73",0,3,"What is the effect of a ReadOnly lock on a resource?",["Users can change it but not delete it","Only Owners can read the resource","It is moved to the Archive tier","Users can read it but not change or delete it"],3,"ReadOnly allows reads only, blocking updates and deletes for everyone, including Owners, until the lock is removed. Allowing changes but not deletion describes CanNotDelete.","Describe resource locks",["Allowing changes but blocking deletion describes a CanNotDelete lock, not ReadOnly.","A ReadOnly lock does not restrict reading to Owners; anyone with read access can still read.","Locks have nothing to do with storage access tiers such as Archive.",null],1],["azf74",0,3,"A user with the Owner role tries to delete a VM that has a CanNotDelete lock. What happens?",["The delete succeeds because of the role","The delete fails until the lock is removed","The VM is deallocated instead","The lock turns into a ReadOnly lock"],1,"Locks apply to all users regardless of role. An Owner can remove the lock first and then delete, but the delete itself fails while the lock is in place.","Describe resource locks",["Locks apply regardless of role, so being Owner does not bypass a CanNotDelete lock.",null,"A lock does not convert a delete into a deallocation; the request is simply rejected.","Locks never change type on their own; the CanNotDelete lock stays as it is until someone removes it."],3],["azf75",0,3,"An auditor asks for Microsoft's independent audit reports, such as SOC and ISO 27001, covering Azure. Where should the company get them?",["Azure Advisor","Microsoft Defender for Cloud","The Service Trust Portal","The Azure Pricing Calculator"],2,"The Service Trust Portal publishes Microsoft's audit reports, certifications and compliance documents. Defender for Cloud shows your own resources' compliance, not Microsoft's audit reports.","Describe the Service Trust Portal",["Azure Advisor gives recommendations for your resources, not Microsoft's independent audit reports.","Defender for Cloud shows your own resources' compliance posture, not Microsoft's third-party audit reports.",null,"The Pricing Calculator estimates costs and holds no compliance documents."],2],["azf76",0,3,"An admin on a borrowed laptop must run Azure CLI commands right away and cannot install any software. What should they use?",["Azure Cloud Shell","Azure PowerShell module","Azure Arc agent","Azure Storage Explorer"],0,"Cloud Shell is a browser-based shell with Azure CLI and Azure PowerShell already installed and authenticated. The PowerShell module and Storage Explorer need a local install.","Describe tools for interacting with Azure",[null,"The Azure PowerShell module must be installed locally, which the admin cannot do, and it is not the Azure CLI.","The Azure Arc agent connects servers to Azure for management; it does not give the admin a shell.","Storage Explorer is a desktop app that needs installing and manages storage, not general CLI commands."],1],["azf77",0,3,"Which shells can Azure Cloud Shell run?",["Bash and PowerShell","Only Command Prompt","Only Bash","Zsh and Command Prompt"],0,"Cloud Shell offers a choice of Bash or PowerShell, both with Azure CLI and Azure PowerShell tools available. It does not run the Windows Command Prompt.","Describe tools for interacting with Azure",[null,"Cloud Shell does not offer the Windows Command Prompt at all.","Bash is available, but PowerShell is also offered, so 'only Bash' is incomplete.","Cloud Shell offers Bash and PowerShell as its shell choices, not Zsh or Command Prompt."],1],["azf78",0,3,"A team's automation scripts already use cmdlets such as New-AzVM and Get-AzResourceGroup. Which tool are they using?",["Azure CLI","Azure Resource Graph","Azure PowerShell","Azure Arc"],2,"Cmdlets in the Verb-AzNoun form belong to the Azure PowerShell Az module. The Azure CLI uses commands such as az vm create instead.","Describe tools for interacting with Azure",["The Azure CLI uses commands that start with az, such as az vm create, not Verb-AzNoun cmdlets.","Azure Resource Graph is a query service for exploring resources, not a scripting tool with New-AzVM cmdlets.",null,"Azure Arc manages resources outside Azure; it is not a command-line toolset."],1],["azf79",0,3,"A company wants to apply Azure Policy, tags and RBAC to Kubernetes clusters running on-premises and in another cloud, without moving them. What should it use?",["Azure Kubernetes Service fleets","Azure Arc-enabled Kubernetes","Azure Migrate","Azure Container Instances"],1,"Azure Arc connects Kubernetes clusters and servers outside Azure to Azure Resource Manager so they can be governed like Azure resources. AKS would mean running new clusters in Azure.","Describe Azure Arc",["AKS fleets manage AKS clusters in Azure; they would not govern existing clusters on-premises or in another cloud where they are.",null,"Azure Migrate moves workloads into Azure, but the company wants to keep the clusters where they are.","Container Instances runs individual containers in Azure; it cannot manage external Kubernetes clusters."],3],["azf80",0,3,"A team wants to deploy the same environment to dev, test and production from a concise, readable file kept in source control. Which option fits?",["Azure Advisor reports","Service Health alerts","Microsoft Purview scans","Bicep files"],3,"Bicep is a declarative infrastructure-as-code language that compiles to ARM templates, giving repeatable deployments from files in source control. Advisor and Service Health are monitoring tools.","Describe ARM templates and Bicep",["Advisor reports are recommendations; they cannot deploy an environment.","Service Health alerts notify about Azure incidents; they do not define or deploy infrastructure.","Purview scans discover and classify data; they do not deploy resources.",null],2],["azf81",0,3,"In which format are Azure Resource Manager (ARM) templates written?",["YAML","XML","JSON","CSV"],2,"ARM templates are JSON files that declare the resources to deploy. Bicep offers a simpler syntax and is converted to ARM JSON when deployed.","Describe ARM templates and Bicep",["YAML is used by some other tools, but ARM templates themselves are JSON.","ARM templates are not written in XML.",null,"CSV is a flat data format and cannot describe nested resource definitions like ARM templates."],1],["azf82",0,3,"What does it mean that ARM templates are declarative?",["You state the end result, not the steps","You script each API call in order","Resources deploy strictly one by one","Templates can run only in the portal"],0,"With declarative templates you define what should exist and Resource Manager works out dependencies and deploys resources in parallel where possible. Scripting every call in order is the imperative approach.","Describe ARM templates and Bicep",[null,"Scripting each API call in order is the imperative approach, the opposite of declarative.","Resource Manager deploys independent resources in parallel and orders only dependent ones.","Templates can be deployed with the CLI, PowerShell, APIs and pipelines, not only the portal."],1],["azf83",0,3,"A company wants personalized recommendations to improve the reliability, security, performance and cost of its existing Azure resources. Which service provides them?",["Azure Service Health","The TCO Calculator","Microsoft Purview","Azure Advisor"],3,"Azure Advisor analyzes your configuration and usage and recommends improvements in reliability, security, performance, operational excellence and cost. Service Health reports platform incidents, not configuration advice.","Describe Azure Advisor",["Service Health reports Azure incidents and maintenance, not configuration improvements for your resources.","The TCO Calculator estimates migration savings; it does not analyze existing Azure resources.","Purview governs data; it does not recommend reliability, performance or cost improvements.",null],2],["azf84",0,3,"An operations team wants to be notified when an Azure platform outage or planned maintenance affects the services and regions it uses. What should it use?",["Azure Advisor","Azure Service Health","Azure Policy","Cost Management anomaly alerts"],1,"Service Health gives a personalized view of Azure incidents, planned maintenance and advisories and can send alerts. Advisor gives best-practice recommendations rather than incident notices.","Describe Azure Service Health",["Advisor gives best-practice recommendations, not notices about outages or planned maintenance.",null,"Azure Policy enforces resource rules and does not report platform incidents.","Cost Management anomaly alerts flag unexpected spending, not Azure outages or maintenance."],1],["azf85",0,3,"One VM became unreachable. An admin wants to know whether an Azure platform problem is affecting that specific VM. Which part of Service Health shows this?",["Azure status page","Resource Health","Azure Advisor","The Pricing Calculator"],1,"Resource Health shows the current and past health of your individual resources. The public Azure status page shows only broad, widespread outages.","Describe Azure Service Health",["The Azure status page shows broad, widespread outages for everyone, not the health of one specific VM.",null,"Azure Advisor gives recommendations; it does not report whether a platform problem is affecting a resource now.","The Pricing Calculator estimates costs and has no health information."],3],["azf86",0,3,"An admin needs to collect logs from many VMs and services in one place and query them together. Which Azure Monitor feature fits?",["Azure Advisor","Resource locks","Service Trust Portal","Log Analytics"],3,"Log Analytics stores logs in a workspace and lets you query them with Kusto Query Language (KQL). Advisor gives recommendations and does not collect logs.","Describe Azure Monitor",["Azure Advisor makes recommendations and does not collect or query logs.","Resource locks protect resources from deletion or change and have nothing to do with logs.","The Service Trust Portal provides Microsoft's compliance reports, not your own log data.",null],1],["azf87",0,3,"An admin wants an email whenever a production VM's CPU stays above 90% for 10 minutes. What should be configured?",["An Azure Monitor alert rule","An Azure Policy with Deny","A Cost Management budget alert","A CanNotDelete lock"],0,"Azure Monitor alert rules watch metrics or logs and notify an action group (email, SMS and more) when a condition is met. Budgets alert on spending, not performance.","Describe Azure Monitor alerts",[null,"A Deny policy blocks noncompliant deployments; it does not watch CPU metrics or send emails.","A budget alert fires on spending thresholds, not on performance metrics like CPU.","A CanNotDelete lock prevents deletion and does not monitor anything."],1],["azf88",0,3,"Developers want to see their web app's request rates, response times, failures and exceptions, and trace slow requests. Which service fits?",["Azure Service Health","Azure Advisor","Application Insights","Microsoft Purview"],2,"Application Insights is the Azure Monitor feature for application performance monitoring. Service Health covers Azure platform incidents, not the app's own performance.","Describe Application Insights",["Service Health covers Azure platform incidents, not the performance of the developers' own app.","Advisor gives general recommendations and does not trace individual requests or exceptions.",null,"Purview governs data and does not monitor application performance."],2],["azf89",0,3,"A manager wants to see which resource group spent the most last month, broken down by service. Which tool should they open?",["Azure Service Health","The TCO Calculator","Application Insights usage reports","Cost analysis in Cost Management"],3,"Cost analysis shows actual and forecast spend grouped by resource group, service, tag and more. The TCO Calculator is for estimates before migrating, not past spending.","Describe Cost Management",["Service Health reports Azure incidents, not what each resource group spent.","The TCO Calculator estimates future migration savings, not past actual spending.","Application Insights usage reports show how users interact with an app, not Azure costs.",null],3],["azf90",0,3,"Resources look and behave the same whether they were created in the portal, with the Azure CLI or with Azure PowerShell. Why?",["Each tool has its own deployment engine","All requests go through Azure Resource Manager","The portal turns scripts into templates","Cloud Shell copies every change it sees"],1,"Azure Resource Manager is the single deployment and management layer: every tool sends requests to it, so results, RBAC and policy checks are consistent. The tools do not have separate engines.","Describe Azure Resource Manager",["The tools do not have separate deployment engines; they all call the same Azure Resource Manager API.",null,"The portal does not convert scripts into templates; each tool sends its requests directly to Resource Manager.","Cloud Shell is just a shell; it does not copy changes made by other tools."],2],["x1",0,1,"A web app with a 99.95% SLA depends on a database with a 99.99% SLA, and the app fails if either is down. What is the expected availability of the whole solution?",["About 99.94%, lower than either one","99.99%, the higher of the two SLAs","99.95%, the lower of the two SLAs","About 99.97%, the average of the two"],0,"For services that all must work, the composite SLA is the product of the individual SLAs: 99.95% x 99.99% is about 99.94%. Picking the lower SLA (99.95%) ignores that the second dependency adds its own downtime.","Domain 1: Composite SLAs",[null,"The weaker link limits the solution; the combined figure can never exceed either component's SLA.","Tempting, but when both must be up you multiply the SLAs (0.9995 x 0.9999), which gives a figure slightly below the lower one.","Averaging SLAs has no meaning; dependent services combine by multiplication, not by averaging."],3],["x2",0,1,"A web tier runs on two VMs. During peak hours an admin adds six more identical VMs behind the load balancer instead of resizing the existing two. What kind of scaling is this?",["Scaling up (vertical)","Failover to a paired region","Scaling out (horizontal)","Scaling down (vertical)"],2,"Adding more instances of the same size is horizontal scaling, called scaling out. Scaling up is the tempting choice, but it changes the size of a VM rather than the number of VMs.","Domain 1: Scalability",["Scaling up means giving an existing VM more CPU or memory; here the size stays the same and instances are added.","Failover moves a workload to another region after an outage; nothing here involves a second region.",null,"Scaling down reduces the size of an existing VM, the opposite of adding capacity."],2],["x3",0,1,"What does a Microsoft SLA for an Azure service describe?",["A guarantee that the service will never have any downtime at all","The uptime Microsoft commits to and the credit if it is missed","The price per hour for each service tier","The steps customers must take to back up their data"],1,"An SLA states Microsoft's commitment for uptime and connectivity of a service and the service credits customers can claim if it falls short. It never promises zero downtime, which is the most common misreading.","Domain 1: Service-level agreements",["No SLA promises zero downtime; each one states a percentage below 100% and what happens if it is not met.",null,"Prices come from the pricing pages and calculator, not from the SLA.","Backups are the customer's responsibility and are not what an SLA documents."],1],["x4",0,1,"Which responsibility always belongs to the cloud provider, whether the customer uses IaaS, PaaS or SaaS?",["The operating system on each server","The information and data stored in the service","The accounts and identities of end users","The physical hosts, network and datacenter"],3,"In every cloud service type the provider runs the physical datacenter, hosts and network. The operating system is the tempting distractor, but in IaaS the customer still patches and configures it.","Domain 1: Shared responsibility model",["In IaaS the customer manages the guest operating system, so this is not the provider's job in every model.","Data always stays the customer's responsibility, in every service type.","Accounts and identities remain the customer's responsibility, even in SaaS.",null],1],["x5",0,1,"A new company has no datacenter and wants all its workloads hosted on infrastructure the provider owns and shares among many customers. Which cloud model is this?",["Private cloud","Hybrid cloud","Public cloud","On-premises datacenter"],2,"In a public cloud a provider owns the infrastructure and many customers share it, paying for what they use. Hybrid is tempting, but it needs some private or on-premises part, which this company does not have.","Domain 1: Cloud models",["A private cloud is used by a single organization, not shared among many customers.","Hybrid combines on-premises or private resources with public cloud; this company has nothing on-premises.",null,"The company explicitly has no datacenter of its own, so it is not running on-premises."],1],["x6",0,1,"Which statement about a private cloud is correct?",["It serves one organization and can be hosted by a third party","It must sit in the organization's own building","It removes the need to buy or maintain hardware","It runs on public regions that are shared with many other tenants"],0,"A private cloud is used exclusively by one organization; it can run on-site or in a hosting provider's datacenter. Saying it must be on the premises is the tempting mistake, because location is not what defines it.","Domain 1: Cloud models",[null,"A private cloud can also be hosted in a third-party datacenter, as long as it serves only that one organization.","With a private cloud the organization still pays for and maintains the hardware, which is a key difference from public cloud.","Sharing infrastructure with other tenants describes the public cloud, not a private one."],2],["x7",0,1,"A company is launching a product whose demand is very uncertain. Why might a CapEx approach of buying servers up front be risky?",["Purchased servers cannot run business applications","CapEx costs can never be depreciated","Up-front purchases are always billed per second","Money is spent on capacity that may never be used"],3,"Capital expenditure commits money before usage is known, so over-buying wastes it and under-buying limits growth. Depreciation is the tempting distractor, but CapEx assets are in fact depreciated.","Domain 1: CapEx vs OpEx",["Owned servers can run any application; the problem is paying for capacity before demand is known.","CapEx purchases are normally depreciated over time, so this statement is false.","Per-second or per-hour billing is a feature of consumption-based OpEx, not of buying hardware.",null],2],["x8",0,1,"A team deallocates its test VMs every evening and weekend. Why does this reduce the bill, and what is still charged?",["All charges stop, including the managed disks","VM compute stops being billed, but the disks are still charged","Microsoft gives a discount for any VM that runs on a schedule","The VMs are moved to a cheaper region overnight"],1,"With consumption-based billing, a deallocated VM does not incur compute charges, but its disks still hold data and are billed. Assuming every charge stops is the common mistake.","Domain 1: Consumption-based model",["Tempting, but managed disks keep their data while the VM is deallocated and continue to be billed for storage.",null,"There is no automatic scheduling discount; the saving comes only from not paying for compute while deallocated.","Deallocation never moves a VM to another region."],2],["x9",0,1,"A retailer wants response times to stay steady as visitor numbers change during the day. Which cloud capabilities most directly support this predictability of performance?",["Autoscaling and load balancing","Resource locks and tags","Reserved instances and Azure Hybrid Benefit","Service Trust Portal reports"],0,"Performance predictability comes from adding capacity automatically when load grows (autoscaling) and spreading requests across instances (load balancing). Reservations are tempting but address cost, not performance.","Domain 1: Predictability",[null,"Locks prevent accidental changes and tags organize resources; neither adjusts capacity to keep performance steady.","These reduce cost, which helps cost predictability, not performance predictability.","The portal provides compliance documents and has no effect on application performance."],2],["x10",0,1,"Which statement about serverless computing is accurate?",["The code runs without any servers at all","You must choose, install and patch the underlying operating system","Servers exist, but the provider manages and scales them","You pay a fixed monthly fee regardless of use"],2,"Serverless is a form of PaaS where the provider runs, scales and maintains the servers and you supply only the code, typically billed per execution. 'No servers at all' is the tempting but wrong reading of the name.","Domain 1: Serverless",["The name is misleading: code still runs on servers, you just do not manage them.","Choosing and patching the operating system is an IaaS task, not part of serverless.",null,"Serverless is usually billed by executions and running time, not a flat fee."],1],["x11",0,1,"A company runs its application on Azure VMs. Which task is Microsoft responsible for?",["Installing antivirus in the guest operating system","Replacing a failed disk in the physical host","Writing network security group rules for the VMs","Deciding which users may sign in to the VMs"],1,"In IaaS Microsoft owns the physical hosts, storage hardware, network and datacenter, so it replaces failed hardware. Everything from the guest OS upward, including firewall rules and accounts, stays with the customer.","Domain 1: IaaS responsibilities",["The guest operating system and software inside it are the customer's responsibility in IaaS.",null,"Network controls such as NSG rules are configured by the customer.","Accounts and access to the VMs remain the customer's responsibility in every model."],2],["x12",0,1,"A company is choosing between building an HR app on PaaS and subscribing to a ready-made SaaS HR product. What is the main trade-off of the SaaS option?",["More operating system patching for the customer","The customer must buy the servers up front","The data stops being the customer's responsibility","Less control over how the application works"],3,"SaaS is the quickest to adopt and needs the least management, but you can only configure what the provider allows. More patching is the opposite of the truth, because SaaS shifts almost all maintenance to the provider.","Domain 1: Choosing a service type",["In SaaS the provider patches everything below the data and accounts, so there is less patching, not more.","SaaS is subscription-based; the customer buys no servers.","Data remains the customer's responsibility in every service type, including SaaS.",null],2],["x13",0,1,"Which is an example of the manageability benefit of the cloud?",["Keeping all servers in a locked room on-site","Buying all the server hardware five years ahead of expected need","Deploying resources from templates and scaling them automatically","Signing a separate contract for each new VM"],2,"Cloud manageability includes deploying from templates, monitoring health, receiving alerts and scaling automatically, all through the portal, CLI or APIs. Buying hardware ahead is a traditional CapEx practice, not a cloud benefit.","Domain 1: Manageability",["Physical server rooms are an on-premises concern, not a cloud manageability benefit.","Buying ahead is a CapEx planning practice, not a manageability feature of the cloud.",null,"Cloud resources are created on demand under the subscription, with no per-VM contract."],1],["x14",0,1,"After a seasonal peak ends, an admin reduces a scale set from 10 VM instances to 3. What is this called?",["Scaling in","Scaling down","Scaling up","Failing over"],0,"Removing instances is horizontal scaling in, the opposite of scaling out. Scaling down is tempting, but it refers to making a single VM smaller, not to having fewer VMs.","Domain 1: Scalability",[null,"Scaling down reduces the size of individual VMs; here the number of instances changes and their size stays the same.","Scaling up adds CPU or memory to existing VMs, which is the opposite direction and a different dimension.","Failover moves a workload after an outage and has nothing to do with reducing instances."],1],["x15",0,1,"Of IaaS, PaaS and SaaS, which service type leaves the customer with the most management responsibility?",["PaaS","SaaS","All three leave exactly the same","IaaS"],3,"IaaS gives the most control and therefore the most responsibility: the customer manages the operating system, applications and network controls. PaaS is tempting but the provider there also manages the OS and runtime.","Domain 1: Cloud service types",["In PaaS the provider also manages the operating system and runtime, so the customer manages less than in IaaS.","SaaS leaves the customer with the least responsibility, mainly data, accounts and devices.","Responsibility shifts toward the provider as you move from IaaS to PaaS to SaaS, so they are not equal.",null],1],["x16",0,1,"An app runs on one VM, and the business wants it to stay available even if a whole datacenter in the region fails. Which change helps most?",["Resize the single VM to a far larger size with more CPUs, memory and disk","Run several instances across availability zones behind a load balancer","Buy a 3-year reservation for the VM","Add a CanNotDelete lock to the VM"],1,"High availability comes from redundancy: multiple instances in separate availability zones, with a load balancer sending traffic to the healthy ones. Resizing is tempting but still leaves a single point of failure.","Domain 1: High availability",["A bigger VM is still a single instance in one datacenter, so it fails with that datacenter.",null,"A reservation lowers cost but does not change how or where the VM runs.","A lock prevents accidental deletion but gives no protection against a datacenter outage."],3],["x17",0,2,"An Azure service automatically keeps copies of itself in several availability zones of a region, and you do not choose a zone. How is this kind of service described?",["Zone-redundant","Zonal","Non-regional","Geo-redundant"],0,"Zone-redundant services replicate across zones in a region automatically. Zonal is the tempting answer, but zonal means the resource is pinned to one specific zone that you select.","Domain 2: Availability zones",[null,"A zonal service is pinned to one zone that you choose, such as a VM deployed to zone 1.","Non-regional services are not tied to a region at all; this service runs within one region's zones.","Geo-redundancy copies data to a second region, not across zones in the same region."],2],["x18",0,2,"Which statement about Azure availability zones is correct?",["Every Azure region has availability zones","Zones in one region are in different countries","Each zone has independent power, cooling and networking","A zone is a group of subscriptions"],2,"An availability zone is one or more datacenters inside a region with independent power, cooling and networking, so a failure in one zone does not take down the others. Assuming every region has zones is a common mistake.","Domain 2: Availability zones",["Not every region supports availability zones, so you must check before relying on them.","Zones are physically separate locations within the same region, not in different countries.",null,"A zone is a physical location of one or more datacenters, not a billing or management grouping."],1],["x19",0,2,"A company wants to serve customers in mainland China from Azure datacenters located in China. What should it use?",["Azure Government","Azure operated by 21Vianet","An Azure region pair in Asia","Azure Arc in a local datacenter"],1,"Azure in mainland China is a sovereign cloud physically separate from global Azure and operated by 21Vianet. Azure Government is the other sovereign option, but it serves US government customers.","Domain 2: Sovereign regions",["Azure Government is a sovereign cloud for US government agencies, not for China.",null,"Regular global regions outside mainland China do not meet this need; China uses a separately operated cloud.","Arc manages resources from Azure but does not provide Azure datacenters inside China."],1],["x20",0,2,"Two VMs are placed in an availability set in one datacenter. What does this protect them against?",["An outage that takes down the entire datacenter building","A complete regional disaster","An admin deleting a VM by mistake","Rack hardware failures and planned host maintenance"],3,"An availability set spreads VMs across fault domains (separate racks, power and network) and update domains (rebooted at different times). The datacenter outage is the tempting answer, but that needs availability zones.","Domain 2: Availability sets",["Availability sets spread VMs within one datacenter; surviving a datacenter outage needs availability zones.","Region-wide outages need a second region, not an availability set.","Accidental deletion is addressed by resource locks or backups, not availability sets.",null],3],["x21",0,2,"A team wants to run several containerized microservices that scale on HTTP traffic, including down to zero, without creating or managing a Kubernetes cluster. Which service fits best?",["Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS)","Azure Container Instances","Azure Container Apps","Virtual Machine Scale Sets"],2,"Azure Container Apps is a serverless container platform for microservices, with automatic scaling including scale to zero and no cluster to manage. AKS is tempting for microservices but brings cluster management the team wants to avoid.","Domain 2: Containers",["AKS gives full Kubernetes control but the team would still manage the cluster and its node pools, which it wants to avoid.","Container Instances suits single, simple containers or short jobs and lacks the built-in microservice features and traffic-based scaling.",null,"Scale sets run VMs, so the team would have to install and manage a container platform itself."],3],["x22",0,2,"A handful of remote developers need encrypted connections from their own laptops, wherever they are, to an Azure virtual network. What should be set up?",["A point-to-site VPN","A site-to-site VPN","An ExpressRoute circuit","Virtual network peering"],0,"Point-to-site VPN connects individual client devices to a virtual network over an encrypted tunnel. Site-to-site is tempting but connects a whole on-premises network using a VPN device.","Domain 2: VPN Gateway",[null,"Site-to-site links a whole on-premises network through a VPN device, not individual laptops on the move.","ExpressRoute is a private circuit from a datacenter or office via a provider, not something individual laptops connect to.","Peering connects Azure virtual networks to each other, not remote user devices."],2],["x23",0,2,"An admin wants to allow inbound traffic to VMs in a subnet only on port 443 and only from certain IP address ranges. What should be used?",["Azure DNS","A resource lock","Virtual network peering","A network security group"],3,"A network security group holds allow and deny rules based on source, destination, port and protocol and can be applied to subnets or network interfaces. Peering is tempting because it is networking, but it only provides connectivity.","Domain 2: Virtual networks",["Azure DNS resolves names to addresses; it does not allow or block traffic.","Locks protect resources from deletion or change; they do not filter network traffic.","Peering connects two virtual networks but does not filter traffic by port or source.",null],2],["x24",0,2,"A web front end must hand orders to a background worker reliably, so orders are not lost if the worker is busy. Which Azure Storage service is designed for this?",["Table Storage","Queue Storage","Azure Files","Azure Disks"],1,"Queue Storage holds messages that one component adds and another processes later, decoupling the two. Table Storage is tempting because it stores records, but it is a datastore, not a message queue.","Domain 2: Azure Storage services",["Table Storage stores structured NoSQL entities, not messages waiting to be processed.",null,"Azure Files provides SMB or NFS file shares, not message passing between components.","Disks are block storage attached to VMs and cannot pass messages between services."],2],["x25",0,2,"An app needs to store large amounts of structured, non-relational data as key/attribute entities without a fixed schema. Which Azure Storage service fits?",["Table Storage","Queue Storage","Blob Storage Archive tier","Azure Disks"],0,"Table Storage is a NoSQL key/attribute store with a schemaless design. Queue Storage is also part of a storage account but is meant for messaging, not storing entities.","Domain 2: Azure Storage services",[null,"Queue Storage is for messages between components, not for storing and querying records.","The Archive tier is for rarely read blobs that must be rehydrated first, not live structured data.","Disks provide block storage to VMs rather than a key/attribute data store."],1],["x26",0,2,"Which Azure Storage service provides block-level storage volumes that attach to Azure VMs as their OS and data drives?",["Azure Blob Storage","Azure Table Storage","Azure managed disks","Azure Queue Storage"],2,"Azure managed disks are block-level volumes managed by Azure and used by VMs as OS and data disks. Blob Storage is tempting because disks are built on it internally, but you use disks, not blobs, for VM drives.","Domain 2: Azure Storage services",["Blob Storage holds unstructured objects accessed over HTTP, not drives attached to a VM.","Table Storage stores structured NoSQL data, not VM volumes.",null,"Queue Storage passes messages between components and cannot act as a VM drive."],1],["x27",0,2,"Backup files are read rarely, deleted after 45 days, and must be readable immediately when needed. Which blob access tier is the most cost-effective fit?",["Hot","Cool","Archive","Cold"],1,"Cool suits infrequently accessed data kept at least 30 days and stays online for immediate reads. Cold is tempting as it is cheaper to store, but its 90-day minimum means deleting at 45 days adds an early deletion charge.","Domain 2: Storage access tiers",["Hot has the highest storage price and suits frequently accessed data, so it costs more than needed here.",null,"Archive blobs must be rehydrated before reading, which can take hours, so it fails the immediate-access need.","Cold expects data to stay at least 90 days; deleting at 45 days would trigger an early deletion charge."],3],["x28",0,2,"A blob in the Archive tier is needed for an audit. What must happen before it can be read?",["It must be copied to a new region","The storage account must be converted to premium","Nothing, it can be read immediately","It must be rehydrated to an online tier"],3,"Archive data is offline; to read it you rehydrate it to Hot, Cool or Cold (or copy it to an online tier), which can take hours. Assuming it reads immediately is the tempting mistake, since the other tiers do.","Domain 2: Storage access tiers",["Reading archived data requires changing its tier, not moving it to another region.","The account type does not need to change; the blob's tier does.","Archived blobs are offline and cannot be read directly, so waiting for rehydration is unavoidable.",null],2],["x29",0,2,"Critical data must survive the loss of an availability zone in the primary region and also a full outage of that region. Which redundancy option fits?",["Zone-redundant storage (ZRS)","Geo-redundant storage (GRS)","Geo-zone-redundant storage (GZRS)","Locally redundant storage (LRS)"],2,"GZRS replicates across availability zones in the primary region and also to a secondary region. GRS is tempting because it covers a regional outage, but in the primary region it uses LRS, not zones.","Domain 2: Storage redundancy",["ZRS survives a zone failure but keeps all copies in one region, so a regional outage would affect it.","GRS copies to a second region, but in the primary region it keeps copies in only one datacenter, so it is weaker against a zone failure.",null,"LRS keeps all copies in a single datacenter, protecting only against disk or rack failures."],3],["x30",0,2,"Which storage redundancy option keeps three copies of data within a single datacenter and costs the least?",["Locally redundant storage (LRS)","Zone-redundant storage (ZRS)","Geo-redundant storage (GRS)","Read-access geo-redundant storage (RA-GRS)"],0,"LRS replicates data three times within one datacenter in the primary region, making it the lowest-cost option. ZRS also keeps three copies, but spreads them across zones, which is why it costs more.","Domain 2: Storage redundancy",[null,"ZRS spreads copies across availability zones, not within one datacenter, and costs more than LRS.","GRS adds a copy in a second region, so it costs more than LRS.","RA-GRS adds a readable secondary region, which is more expensive than LRS."],1],["x31",0,2,"An admin needs a command-line tool to script copying blobs and files to and between storage accounts. Which tool fits?",["Azure Storage Explorer","Azure Data Box","Azure Migrate","AzCopy"],3,"AzCopy is a command-line utility for copying blobs and files to or from storage accounts and between them. Storage Explorer is tempting, but it is a graphical tool rather than a scriptable one.","Domain 2: Moving data",["Storage Explorer is a graphical app; it is not the tool for scripting copies from the command line.","Data Box is a physical device shipped for very large offline transfers.","Azure Migrate discovers, assesses and migrates servers, not individual blobs.",null],1],["x32",0,2,"A support engineer wants a free graphical app on Windows, macOS or Linux to browse containers, upload files and manage blobs. Which tool fits?",["AzCopy","Azure Storage Explorer","Azure File Sync","Azure Advisor"],1,"Azure Storage Explorer is a standalone graphical app for working with storage accounts on Windows, macOS and Linux. AzCopy handles the same data but only from the command line.","Domain 2: Moving data",["AzCopy is a command-line tool, not a graphical browser for storage.",null,"File Sync caches Azure file shares on Windows servers; it is not a browsing tool.","Advisor gives optimization recommendations and does not manage blob content."],1],["x33",0,2,"Which storage account type does Microsoft recommend for most scenarios, supporting blobs, files, queues and tables?",["Standard general-purpose v2","Premium block blobs","Premium file shares","Premium page blobs"],0,"Standard general-purpose v2 accounts support Blob Storage, Files, Queues and Tables and are recommended for most scenarios. The premium types are tempting for performance but each supports only one kind of data.","Domain 2: Storage accounts",[null,"Premium block blob accounts support only block and append blobs, for low-latency needs.","Premium file share accounts support only Azure Files.","Premium page blob accounts support only page blobs, not queues or tables."],1],["x34",0,2,"Users sign in with Windows Hello for Business or a FIDO2 security key and never type a password. What is this called?",["Single sign-on","Conditional Access","Passwordless authentication","Self-service password reset"],2,"Passwordless methods replace the password with something you have plus something you are or know, such as Windows Hello, FIDO2 keys or the Authenticator app. SSO is tempting, but it is about signing in once, not about removing passwords.","Domain 2: Authentication methods",["SSO lets one sign-in reach many apps but can still use a password for that sign-in.","Conditional Access decides when to require extra checks; it is not a sign-in method.",null,"Password reset still relies on a password, so it is the opposite of passwordless."],1],["x35",0,2,"A retailer wants shoppers to create accounts on its website and sign in with an email address or a social account. What should it use?",["B2B collaboration","Customer identity in Microsoft Entra External ID","Microsoft Entra Domain Services","Azure role-based access control"],1,"Customer identity and access management in Entra External ID lets consumers sign up and sign in to your apps with local or social accounts. B2B collaboration is tempting because it is also external identity, but it is for partner organizations.","Domain 2: External identities",["B2B collaboration invites business partners into the workforce tenant, not the public signing up as customers.",null,"Domain Services provides managed domain join, LDAP and Kerberos, not customer sign-up.","RBAC controls access to Azure resources, not sign-up for a public website."],2],["x36",0,2,"A user is given the Reader role at the subscription scope. What can the user view?",["Only the subscription object itself, but none of its resources","Every subscription in the tenant","Only resources the user created","All resource groups and resources in that subscription"],3,"Azure RBAC assignments are inherited from a parent scope to the resource groups and resources beneath it. Assuming the assignment covers only the subscription object is the tempting mistake.","Domain 2: Azure RBAC",["Role assignments are inherited by child scopes, so resources inside are included.","The assignment applies to one subscription and its children, not to other subscriptions.","RBAC scope is based on where the role is assigned, not on who created a resource.",null],2],["x37",0,2,"Granting just-in-time and just-enough access, and nothing more, is part of which Zero Trust principle?",["Verify explicitly","Assume breach","Use least privilege access","Trust the corporate network"],2,"Zero Trust's three principles are verify explicitly, use least privilege access and assume breach; just-in-time and just-enough access is least privilege. Verify explicitly is tempting but concerns how requests are authenticated.","Domain 2: Zero Trust",["Verify explicitly is about authenticating and authorizing based on all available signals, not limiting permissions.","Assume breach is about minimizing blast radius and segmenting access, not specifically the amount of access granted.",null,"Zero Trust explicitly rejects automatically trusting any network."],1],["x38",0,2,"In the defense-in-depth model, which layer includes DDoS protection and filtering attacks before they reach your network?",["Perimeter","Data","Application","Identity and access"],0,"The perimeter layer uses DDoS protection and perimeter firewalls to stop network attacks before they reach resources. The network layer is nearby but limits communication between resources inside, which is why perimeter is the better fit.","Domain 2: Defense in depth",[null,"The data layer protects stored information, for example with encryption, not incoming attack traffic.","The application layer focuses on secure code and app vulnerabilities, not large-scale network attacks.","This layer controls authentication and authorization, not filtering of network floods."],2],["x39",0,2,"An admin deletes the resource group RG-Test. What happens to the VMs and storage accounts inside it?",["They move to the default resource group","They keep running without a group","The delete fails while the group contains resources","They are deleted along with the group"],3,"Deleting a resource group deletes every resource in it, which is why resources with the same lifecycle are grouped together. Expecting the delete to fail is tempting, but only a resource lock would stop it.","Domain 2: Resource groups",["There is no default group; resources cannot exist outside a resource group.","Every resource must belong to exactly one resource group, so they cannot remain groupless.","Azure does not require an empty group; deleting it removes everything in it unless a lock blocks it.",null],1],["x40",0,2,"Which two boundaries does an Azure subscription provide?",["A network boundary and an encryption boundary","A billing boundary and an access control boundary","A region boundary and an availability zone boundary","A tag boundary and a lock boundary"],1,"Each subscription gets its own invoice and cost reports and can have its own access policies, making it a billing and access control boundary. A region boundary is tempting, but a subscription can span any regions.","Domain 2: Subscriptions",["Networks are defined by virtual networks, and encryption is configured per service, not by subscription.",null,"One subscription can hold resources in many regions and zones.","Tags and locks can be applied at many scopes; they are not what a subscription defines."],2],["x41",0,3,"A security lead says: 'We need to stop anyone from creating VMs without an Owner tag, even people who have permission to create VMs.' Which tool meets this, and why?",["Azure Policy, because it controls resource properties","Azure RBAC, because it controls user actions","A resource lock, because it blocks changes","Azure Advisor, because it gives recommendations"],0,"Azure Policy evaluates resource properties such as tags and can deny noncompliant deployments regardless of the user's role. RBAC is the tempting answer, but it controls who can perform actions, not what the resources look like.","Domain 3: Azure Policy vs RBAC",[null,"RBAC decides whether a user may create VMs at all; it cannot require a tag on the VM being created.","Locks stop deletion or changes to existing resources, not creation of new resources without tags.","Advisor only recommends; it does not block noncompliant deployments."],3],["x42",0,3,"A CanNotDelete lock is placed on a resource group. Next week, a new VM is created in that group. What applies to the new VM?",["The lock covers only resources present when it was created","The VM gets a ReadOnly lock instead","It inherits the lock and cannot be deleted","The VM cannot be created while the lock exists"],2,"A lock at a parent scope is inherited by all resources in that scope, including ones added later. Thinking the VM cannot be created is tempting, but CanNotDelete still allows creating and updating.","Domain 3: Resource locks",["Locks apply to the scope, so resources added later inherit them too.","Inheritance keeps the same lock type; it does not switch it to ReadOnly.",null,"CanNotDelete allows creating and changing resources; it only blocks deletion."],2],["x43",0,3,"Which built-in roles can create or delete resource locks by default?",["Contributor","Owner and User Access Administrator","Reader","Any user who can see the resource"],1,"Managing locks requires Microsoft.Authorization/locks permissions, which among built-in roles only Owner and User Access Administrator have. Contributor is tempting because it can manage resources, but it cannot manage authorization objects such as locks.","Domain 3: Resource locks",["Contributor can manage resources but cannot manage locks, which need Microsoft.Authorization permissions.",null,"Reader can only view resources and cannot create or remove locks.","Viewing a resource grants no right to add or remove locks on it."],2],["x44",0,3,"A policy that denies a certain VM size is assigned to a subscription that already has VMs of that size. What happens to the existing VMs?",["They are deleted automatically","They are resized to an allowed size","They are shut down until a policy exemption is created","They keep running and are reported as noncompliant"],3,"The deny effect blocks new or updated deployments that break the rule, while existing resources are evaluated and shown as noncompliant. Automatic resizing is tempting, but the deny effect does not modify existing resources.","Domain 3: Azure Policy",["Azure Policy never deletes existing resources.","Deny does not change existing resources; it only blocks new or updated deployments.","Policy does not stop running VMs, and exemptions only change how compliance is reported.",null],3],["x45",0,3,"A policy assignment is made at a management group that contains three subscriptions. Where does it apply?",["Only to the management group object itself","Only to subscriptions created after the assignment","To all three subscriptions and their resources","Only to the first subscription in the list"],2,"Policy assigned at a management group is inherited by all subscriptions, resource groups and resources beneath it. Believing it covers only new subscriptions is tempting but wrong: existing ones are evaluated too.","Domain 3: Azure Policy scope",["Policy assignments are inherited by every scope under the management group.","Existing subscriptions under the group are included as well as new ones.",null,"Assignments are not limited to one child; they flow to all of them."],2],["x46",0,3,"A user shuts down a VM from inside Windows. The portal then shows its status as 'Stopped', not 'Stopped (deallocated)'. Is compute still being billed?",["Yes, until the VM is deallocated","No, any stopped VM costs nothing","No, but the disks are deleted","Yes, but only at half the normal rate"],0,"A VM stopped from inside the guest OS keeps its compute resources allocated and continues to be billed; stopping it from Azure deallocates it. Assuming any stopped VM is free is the tempting error.","Domain 3: Factors that affect cost",[null,"Only deallocated VMs stop incurring compute charges; a VM stopped inside the OS still holds its hardware.","Shutting down never deletes managed disks, and disks are billed either way.","There is no half rate; compute is billed normally while the VM stays allocated."],3],["x47",0,3,"A nightly batch rendering job can be interrupted and restarted without harm. Which VM purchase option usually costs the least?",["Pay-as-you-go VMs","Dedicated hosts","A reservation for an always-on VM","Azure Spot Virtual Machines"],3,"Spot VMs use spare Azure capacity at a deep discount and can be evicted when Azure needs it, which suits interruptible work. A reservation is tempting for savings but assumes steady use around the clock.","Domain 3: Factors that affect cost",["Pay-as-you-go has no discount, so it costs more than using spare capacity.","Dedicated hosts give you a whole physical server, which adds cost rather than reducing it.","Reservations suit steady, always-on use; a nightly interruptible job fits spare capacity better.",null],2],["x48",0,3,"A company wants to find VMs with consistently low CPU usage so it can resize or shut them down to save money. Which service gives these recommendations?",["Azure Service Health","Azure Advisor","The TCO Calculator","Microsoft Purview"],1,"Azure Advisor analyzes usage and recommends cost savings such as resizing or shutting down underused VMs. The TCO Calculator is tempting because it deals with savings, but it compares on-premises costs with Azure.","Domain 3: Azure Advisor",["Service Health reports Azure platform incidents and maintenance, not your utilization.",null,"The TCO Calculator estimates savings from migrating on-premises servers, not from resizing Azure VMs.","Purview governs and classifies data; it does not analyze VM usage."],2],["x49",0,3,"A script contains commands such as 'az group create' and 'az vm list'. Which tool does it use?",["Azure CLI","Azure PowerShell","Azure Storage Explorer","Bicep"],0,"Commands beginning with 'az' belong to the Azure CLI, which runs on Windows, macOS and Linux and in Cloud Shell. Azure PowerShell is tempting as the other scripting tool, but it uses cmdlets like Get-AzVM.","Domain 3: Tools for Azure",[null,"Azure PowerShell uses verb-noun cmdlets such as New-AzResourceGroup, not 'az' commands.","Storage Explorer is a graphical app, not a command-line tool.","Bicep is a declarative template language, not a set of imperative commands like these."],1],["x50",0,3,"A manager with no scripting experience wants a browser-based way to create resources and build custom dashboards showing their status. What should they use?",["Azure CLI","ARM templates","The Azure portal","AzCopy"],2,"The Azure portal is a web-based console for creating and managing resources, with customizable dashboards. The CLI is tempting since it also manages everything, but it is text-based.","Domain 3: Tools for Azure",["The CLI requires typing commands, which the manager wants to avoid.","Templates are JSON files for deployments, not a graphical dashboard.",null,"AzCopy only copies data to and from storage."],1],["x51",0,3,"A company wants its on-premises Windows and Linux servers to appear as Azure resources so it can apply Azure Policy and tags to them without migrating them. What should it use?",["Azure Migrate","Azure Arc-enabled servers","Azure Virtual Desktop","Azure Data Box"],1,"Azure Arc-enabled servers project machines outside Azure into Azure Resource Manager so you can govern them with Policy, RBAC and tags. Azure Migrate is tempting but it is for moving servers into Azure.","Domain 3: Azure Arc",["Azure Migrate moves servers into Azure, but the servers here must stay on-premises.",null,"Virtual Desktop delivers desktops to users and does not project servers into Azure.","Data Box is a device for shipping large amounts of data offline."],2],["x52",0,3,"A template deploys a VM that depends on a virtual network and a storage account. How does Azure Resource Manager handle the deployment?",["It deploys every resource one at a time, in file order","It requires a separate template for each resource","It fails unless the admin creates the network manually first","It deploys dependencies first and others in parallel"],3,"Resource Manager orchestrates deployments, creating resources in the order their dependencies require and deploying independent ones in parallel. Assuming strict file order is tempting, but order comes from dependencies.","Domain 3: Azure Resource Manager",["Resource Manager does not simply follow file order; it uses dependencies and runs independent resources in parallel.","One template can describe many resources and their dependencies.","Resource Manager creates the network itself when it is defined in the template.",null],2],["x53",0,3,"How does Bicep relate to ARM templates?",["Bicep replaces Azure Resource Manager","Bicep can only deploy resources outside Azure","Bicep files are converted into ARM template JSON","Bicep is an imperative scripting language"],2,"Bicep is a more concise declarative language that is transpiled into ARM template JSON before deployment. Saying it replaces Resource Manager is tempting, but Resource Manager still performs every deployment.","Domain 3: Infrastructure as code",["Bicep deployments still go through Azure Resource Manager like any other request.","Bicep is specifically a language for deploying Azure resources.",null,"Bicep is declarative; you describe the desired resources, not the steps."],2],["x54",0,3,"Dev, test and production were built by hand and now differ in small ways that cause bugs. What approach best prevents this in future?",["Define the environments as infrastructure as code","Write more detailed manual build instructions for each environment","Give every engineer Owner rights","Apply ReadOnly locks after each build"],0,"Infrastructure as code, such as Bicep or ARM templates in source control, deploys the same definition repeatedly for consistent environments. More detailed manual instructions are tempting but still leave room for human variation.","Domain 3: Infrastructure as code",[null,"Manual steps still vary between people and runs, so drift continues.","Broader permissions do not make environments consistent and weaken security.","ReadOnly locks block needed changes and do not make environments identical."],2],["x55",0,3,"Someone without an Azure account wants to check whether there is a widespread Azure outage right now. Where should they look?",["Azure Service Health","Resource Health","Azure Advisor","The Azure status page"],3,"The public Azure status page shows broad service health across regions and needs no sign-in. Service Health is tempting, but it gives a personalized view for your own subscriptions inside the portal.","Domain 3: Azure Service Health",["Service Health is personalized to your subscriptions and needs sign-in to the portal.","Resource Health shows the health of your individual resources and needs access to them.","Advisor gives optimization recommendations, not outage information.",null],2],["x56",0,3,"Which Azure Monitor data type holds numeric values, such as CPU percentage, collected at regular intervals and suited to near real-time charts?",["Logs","Metrics","Resource locks","Tags"],1,"Azure Monitor Metrics are numeric values sampled at regular intervals and are ideal for fast charts and alerts. Logs are tempting but contain records of many types analyzed with queries.","Domain 3: Azure Monitor",["Logs hold records with varied data types for detailed querying, not just regularly sampled numbers.",null,"Locks protect resources from deletion or change and are not monitoring data.","Tags are name/value labels for organizing resources, not performance data."],1],["x57",0,3,"When an alert fires, the on-call team must receive an SMS and an email, and a webhook must call the ticketing system. What defines these notifications and actions?",["An action group","A resource group","A management group","An Azure Policy initiative"],0,"Azure Monitor alert rules use action groups to list who is notified (SMS, email, push) and which actions run, such as webhooks. Resource group is tempting only because of the similar name.","Domain 3: Azure Monitor alerts",[null,"A resource group is a container for resources, not a list of notification targets.","Management groups organize subscriptions for governance, not alert notifications.","Initiatives group policy definitions and do not send alert notifications."],2],["x58",0,3,"Which language is used to query data in a Log Analytics workspace?",["Bicep","JSON","Kusto Query Language (KQL)","PowerShell cmdlets only"],2,"Log Analytics uses the Kusto Query Language to search, filter and summarize log data. JSON is tempting because it appears throughout Azure, but it is a data format, not a query language.","Domain 3: Log Analytics",["Bicep is for defining Azure resources to deploy, not for querying logs.","JSON is a data format used by ARM templates, not a query language for logs.",null,"Log Analytics queries are written in KQL, even though PowerShell can submit them."],1],["x59",0,3,"Two storage accounts hold the same amount of data, but one costs noticeably more. It uses GRS and the Hot tier, while the cheaper one uses LRS and the Cool tier. What explains the difference?",["Only the region affects storage price","Redundancy and access tier both affect storage price","Storage is billed only by the number of read and write transactions","The Hot tier is always cheaper for storing data"],1,"Storage cost depends on capacity, redundancy (GRS copies to a second region and costs more than LRS) and tier (Hot has higher storage prices than Cool). Transaction cost is the tempting distractor, but it is only one part of the bill.","Domain 3: Factors that affect cost",["Region matters, but redundancy and tier also change the price even within one region.",null,"Capacity, redundancy and tier drive the price as well as transactions.","Hot has the highest capacity price; it is cheaper only for access, not for storing."],3],["x60",0,3,"A team sets a monthly budget in Cost Management with alerts at 80% and 100%. Spending reaches 100%. What happens by default?",["All resources in the scope are stopped","New deployments are blocked for the month","The subscription is converted to a free account","Alerts are sent; resources keep running"],3,"Cost Management budgets monitor spending and send alerts at thresholds, but they do not stop or block resources on their own. Expecting resources to be stopped is the tempting assumption.","Domain 3: Cost Management budgets",["Budgets do not stop resources; they notify, and any automation must be set up separately.","Budgets do not block deployments; that would need Azure Policy or other controls.","Budgets never change the subscription's offer type.",null],3]]);
